  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Syria Coastal Area Response</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Under the Water Sanitation and Hygiene (WASH) sector, several critical interventions are planned: these include the supply and installation of two generators in Lattakia City, benefiting approximately 15,000 people. Additionally, a major rehabilitation of the pumping station in Bahlwlieh Rastin (Phases 1 and 2) is proposed. Operational and maintenance support for water systems in both Latakia and Tartous is targeting 50,000 beneficiaries. To improve water storage at the household level, the distribution of 1,000 water tanks is planned in Jableh, Bahloleyh, and Haffeh, expected to reach 5,000 individuals.  In support of infection prevention and control (IPC) in healthcare settings, four hospitals across the coastal area (including the national hospital in Jableh, and facilities in Qerdaha and Haffeh) will receive hygiene items, cleaning materials, and Personal Protective Equipment (PPEs). Family hygiene kits will be distributed to 3,000 households in Bahloleyh, Haffeh, Jableh, and Latakia, benefiting 15,000 people Further distributions include 2,000 menstrual hygiene management (MHM) kits for women and girls, and 500 hygiene kits tailored for people with special needs (PSNs), including the elderly and individuals with incontinence. These distributions will take place in the same WASH -targeted locations.  Within the Protection sector, individual protection assistance will be provided to 200 cases across all locations. In addition, 500 kits will be distributed to pregnant and lactating women (PLW), particularly in Jableh and Beit Yashout.. Case management, psychosocial support (PSS), and protection awareness activities are also planned, expected to benefit 5,000 individuals, although this remains unfunded. For Food Security and Livelihoods (FSL), a Multi -Purpose Cash Assistance (MPCA) intervention targeting 1,000 households is planned in Jableh, Al -Haffa, and Kurdiyeh, aiming to reach 5,000 individuals.</narrative>
  </description>
